    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable delete', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'For Juniper devices, the equivalent command to Cisco\\'s `no shutdown` is `delete disable` or simply `set <interface> disable` (when enabling an interface, you remove the disable flag). Here\\'s the translation:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable delete\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if the interface was previously configured as disabled and you want to explicitly enable it, you could also use:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 description \"Active interface\"\\n##\\n\\n(Note: Juniper interfaces are enabled by default unless explicitly configured with `disable`, so simply removing the disable flag is sufficient.)', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable delete'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 description \"Active interface\"', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'For Juniper devices, the equivalent command to Cisco\\'s `no shutdown` is `delete disable` or simply `set <interface> disable` (when enabling an interface, you remove the disable flag). Here\\'s the translation:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable delete\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if the interface was previously configured as disabled and you want to explicitly enable it, you could also use:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 description \"Active interface\"\\n##\\n\\n(Note: Juniper interfaces are enabled by default unless explicitly configured with `disable`, so simply removing the disable flag is sufficient.)', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 description \"Active interface\"'}]]": 1,
